Reasoning through the schema:

**Entity types in the schema.** Each record is a conflict event (ACLED). Distinct real-world entities visible on the row: (1) the event itself, (2) actor1, (3) actor2, (4) the event location, (5) the admin1 region containing it, (6) the country, (7) the world region, (8) the geographic point (latitude/longitude), and (9) the fatality effect of the event.

**Event → bsm:Action.** The ontology's Action class carries eventStartDateTime, actionDescriptionText, classificationCode, involvedActor, hasResult, and (inherited) hasGeographicLocation — exactly the shape of an ACLED event row. Identifier: event_id_cnty, which is unique and stable per its description, so the subject template is event_{event_id_cnty}.

**Actors → bsm:Actor.** actor1/actor2 are described as "main actors involved in the event," and Action has the object property involvedActor leading to Actor, so each becomes its own subject_mapping linked by an iri value. I use bsm:Actor rather than bsm:Organisation or bsm:Person because sample values mix organisations ("Military Forces of Poland (2023-)") with non-organisational groups ("Unidentified Armed Group (Baltic)", and per the description "Civilians (Kenya)", "Rioters"). Subjects: actor_{actor1} and actor_{actor2}; actor2 may be blank, in which case that entity is skipped for the record — acceptable. On each actor: the name column maps to bsm:conceptName; inter1/inter2 ("a text value indicating the type of Actor 1") maps to bsm:typeName; assoc_actor_1/2 holds semicolon-separated associated actors or further designations — no structured property fits a multi-value blend like that, so I keep it as a literal bsm:additionalInformationText with low confidence rather than minting broken bundle-IRIs.

**Location hierarchy → bsm:Location.** Action (via BattlespaceConcept) has hasGeographicLocation to Location, and Location has locationName plus inherited partOf — the schema's location/admin1/country/region columns evidence a containment chain, so I model four Location subjects: location_{country}_{admin1}_{location} partOf admin1_{country}_{admin1} partOf country_{country} partOf region_{region}, each with locationName from its own column. admin2 and admin3 are often blank; since any empty column in an expression silently drops the entity, I exclude them from the chain and leave them unmapped. iso is a single ISO-3166 code column — the AlternateIdentifier class models value-plus-scheme structure, which one generic code column does not evidence, so iso stays unmapped per the bundling-class warning.

**Point → bsm:AbsolutePoint.** Location has hasGeometry to Geometry; latitude/longitude are decimal degrees EPSG:4326, matching AbsolutePoint's latitudeDecimal/longitudeDecimal (WGS84) exactly. Subject: point_{latitude}_{longitude}, referenced from the location's hasGeometry.

**Fatalities → bsm:ActionEffect.** Action has hasResult, and ActionEffect has effectQuantityNumber — "number of reported fatalities arising from an event" is a quantitative effect of the action. Subject: effect_{event_id_cnty} (one per event), with effectQuantityNumber ← fatalities. I do not invent an effectDescriptionCode constant.

**Remaining event columns.** notes ("a short description of the event") → bsm:actionDescriptionText. tags ("additional structured information") → bsm:additionalInformationText. event_id_cnty is also mapped as a literal to bsm:sourceIdentifier (the source system's identifier). event_date → bsm:eventStartDateTime, but explicitly ambiguous: the ontology splits start vs end (eventStartDateTime vs eventEndDateTime) and the single date column is silent on the qualifier; I choose start only because an event's date conventionally marks its beginning, at reduced confidence. event_type ("Battles") → bsm:classificationCode with a noted qualifier mismatch: the values are labels, not codes, but it is the only typing property on Action.

**Unmapped.** year (redundant with event_date, no year property), time_precision, disorder_type and sub_event_type (no second classification property; hasSubAction would invent action entities from mere labels), interaction (derived composite of inter1/inter2), civilian_targeting (hasFocus is too speculative), iso, admin2, admin3, geo_precision, source and source_scale (no source-name property; sourceIdentifier is for identifiers), timestamp (upload time; observationDateTime belongs to Observation, which this is not), and population_1km/2km/5km/best (no demographic properties exist).

**IRI consistency check.** Every iri-typed value's transformation is copied character-for-character from its target's subject_transformation: actor_{actor1}, actor_{actor2}, effect_{event_id_cnty}, location_{country}_{admin1}_{location}, point_{latitude}_{longitude}, admin1_{country}_{admin1}, country_{country}, region_{region}. All class and property URIs appear verbatim in the ontology. No empty transformation objects are emitted.

